UNITS = [
    {
        'id':'claims-argument-structure','slug':'claims-argument-structure','title':'Claims & Argument Structure','skills':['R5.1','R5.2'],
        'goal':'Identify an author\'s main claim, distinguish supporting claims from evidence, and trace how the parts of an argument build toward a conclusion.',
        'explain':'An argument is more than a topic or opinion. The main claim is the position the writer wants the reader to accept. Supporting claims give reasons for that position, and evidence gives facts, examples, or data that support those reasons. Reading the argument as a chain helps you see what each sentence is doing.',
        'contrast':'Do not confuse a claim with evidence. “The city should add shade at the busiest stops” is a claim. “Ridership at those stops is highest between 2:00 and 5:00 p.m.” is evidence. Also do not choose a broad topic such as “bus stops” when the question asks for the writer\'s actual position.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “What is the author\'s main claim?”, “Which statement is a supporting claim?”, “What role does this detail play?”, or “How does the author develop the argument?”',
        'method':['State the issue in a few words.','Find the sentence or idea the writer most wants you to accept.','Group the reasons underneath that claim.','Label facts, examples, and data as evidence rather than claims.','Check that your answer describes the whole argument, not one paragraph.'],
        'example_text':'A college proposes keeping the library open one hour later during exam weeks. A student survey found that 64 percent of respondents had difficulty finding quiet study space after 8 p.m. The proposal recommends a four-week pilot before any permanent schedule change.',
        'example_q':'Which statement best expresses the main claim?','example_reason':'The survey is evidence and the four-week pilot is a detail about implementation. The central position is that the college should test later library hours during exam weeks.','example_answer':'The college should test extending library hours during exam weeks.',
        'traps':['Choosing the topic instead of the claim.','Treating a statistic or example as if it were the writer\'s position.','Choosing a supporting reason that is narrower than the whole argument.'],
        'diagnostic':['If you chose a number or factual detail, ask whether it is evidence rather than a claim.','If your answer covers only one paragraph, zoom out and ask what the entire passage is trying to establish.','If two claims seem possible, look for the one the other claims support.'],
        'quick_text':'A manager argues that a six-week flexible-start pilot should continue because late arrivals fell and customer coverage remained stable.','quick_q':'What is the main claim?','quick_answer':'The six-week flexible-start pilot should continue; the attendance and coverage results are supporting evidence.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'A school newsletter says the campus should add two refill stations. It notes that students bought 4,200 bottled drinks last semester and that the existing station is busiest at lunch.','task':'Which statement is the main claim?','choices':['Students bought 4,200 bottled drinks.','The campus should add two refill stations.','The existing station is busiest at lunch.','Lunch is the busiest time on campus.'],'answer':'B. The campus should add two refill stations.','why':'That is the position the writer wants accepted. The purchase total and usage detail are evidence.'},
            {'text':'A supervisor recommends a short safety refresher before the busy season. She says new workers made most checklist errors last year, and a 20-minute refresher could review the three most missed steps.','task':'Which statement is a supporting claim?','choices':['The company exists.','A refresher could target the steps workers miss most often.','Last year had twelve months.','Safety matters in every workplace.'],'answer':'B. A refresher could target the steps workers miss most often.','why':'It gives a reason the proposed refresher could be useful; it supports the recommendation.'},
            {'text':'A council member proposes testing weekend bus service. First, she explains that hospital staff report few transport options on Sundays. Next, she cites ridership estimates. Finally, she asks for a three-month trial.','task':'How is the argument organized?','choices':['Problem, supporting evidence, proposed test.','Chronological history of the bus company.','Two unrelated opinions followed by a definition.','A cause is stated and then disproved.'],'answer':'A. Problem, supporting evidence, proposed test.','why':'Each part performs a clear role in building the recommendation.'},
            {'text':'“The park should keep one gate open until 9 p.m. because evening walkers currently exit through an unlit side path.”','task':'Which part is evidence rather than the claim?','choices':['The park should keep one gate open until 9 p.m.','Evening walkers currently exit through an unlit side path.','The park has gates.','Walking is healthy.'],'answer':'B. Evening walkers currently exit through an unlit side path.','why':'It is a factual condition offered to support the recommendation.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A town planner argues for adding shade near the two busiest transit stops. She cites rider counts, afternoon temperature readings, and a survey in which riders named heat as a reason they avoid waiting for the bus. She does not propose covering every stop.','task':'Which statement best captures the argument chain?','choices':['Heat exists, so every stop should receive a shelter immediately.','The busiest stops have measurable heat exposure and rider concern, so targeted shade should be tested there first.','Riders dislike buses, so the town should reduce service.','Temperature readings alone prove shade will increase ridership.'],'answer':'B. The busiest stops have measurable heat exposure and rider concern, so targeted shade should be tested there first.','why':'It preserves the targeted scope and connects the evidence to the recommendation without overstating what the data prove.'},
            {'text':'A principal supports a phone-free lunch pilot. She says students report wanting more face-to-face conversation, teachers observed fewer lunchroom disputes during two trial days, and the school should test the policy for one month before deciding whether to keep it.','task':'Which statement is a supporting claim rather than the main claim?','choices':['The school should test a phone-free lunch policy for one month.','Reducing phone use at lunch may encourage more direct student interaction.','Teachers work at the school.','The school should permanently ban phones everywhere.'],'answer':'B. Reducing phone use at lunch may encourage more direct student interaction.','why':'It is a reason supporting the one-month pilot. The pilot itself is the main recommendation.'},
            {'text':'An article argues that a river trail should be repaired in stages. Paragraph 2 describes erosion near the northern section. Paragraph 3 estimates repair costs. Paragraph 4 recommends fixing the highest-risk segment first and monitoring the rest.','task':'What role does Paragraph 3 play?','choices':['It provides cost information that helps justify a staged repair.','It changes the topic from trail repair to tourism.','It proves the northern section is the only damaged area.','It states the main claim without support.'],'answer':'A. It provides cost information that helps justify a staged repair.','why':'The cost estimate helps explain why the author recommends prioritizing rather than repairing everything at once.'},
            {'text':'A company memo argues for rotating one remote-work day among departments. It cites desk-capacity limits, reports that customer response times were unchanged during a trial, and notes that teams can choose different days.','task':'Which option is the best concise thesis?','choices':['Remote work is popular.','The company has desks.','A rotating remote-work day can reduce space pressure without reducing customer coverage, so the company should continue a controlled version of the policy.','Every employee should work remotely whenever desired.'],'answer':'C. A rotating remote-work day can reduce space pressure without reducing customer coverage, so the company should continue a controlled version of the policy.','why':'It captures the position, the central reasons, and the limited scope.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'finding-evidence','slug':'finding-evidence','title':'Finding Evidence','skills':['R5.3'],
        'goal':'Locate the specific sentence, fact, example, or data point that most directly supports a stated claim.',
        'explain':'Evidence answers the question, “What in the source gives us a reason to believe this claim?” Strong evidence is not merely related to the topic. It connects directly to the exact claim being tested.',
        'contrast':'A relevant detail may still be weaker than a more direct piece of evidence. Also separate evidence from the author\'s recommendation, explanation, or prediction.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “Which detail best supports the claim?”, “Which evidence is most direct?”, “Which detail would be least useful?”, or “Which additional evidence would strengthen the argument?”',
        'method':['Underline the exact claim in the question.','Turn the claim into a test: what would I need to see to believe this?','Scan the source for facts or examples that answer that test.','Prefer direct evidence over background information.','Re-read the claim and evidence together to confirm the connection.'],
        'example_text':'A tutoring center says evening hours improve access. During the pilot, 41 percent of evening visitors reported that daytime work or family duties had prevented them from attending earlier sessions.','example_q':'Which detail most directly supports the access claim?','example_reason':'The 41 percent figure directly connects evening attendance to barriers that existed during daytime hours.','example_answer':'The report that 41 percent of evening visitors could not attend earlier because of work or family duties.',
        'traps':['Choosing a true detail that does not support the exact claim.','Choosing the author\'s conclusion instead of the evidence behind it.','Picking dramatic information when a quieter detail is more directly relevant.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your choice is only about the general topic, restate the exact claim before looking again.','If your answer sounds like advice or a prediction, check whether it is actually evidence.','If two details fit, ask which one most directly measures or demonstrates the claim.'],
        'quick_text':'A pilot cafeteria line opened ten minutes earlier. Average first-period tardiness among students who used the cafeteria fell from 14 to 7 cases per week.','quick_q':'What evidence most directly supports the claim that the earlier opening may reduce tardiness?','quick_answer':'The drop from 14 to 7 weekly tardiness cases among cafeteria users.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'A community center claims its new Saturday hours improve access for working adults. Forty-six percent of Saturday visitors reported that they could not attend weekday daytime programs.','task':'Which detail best supports the claim?','choices':['The center has a front desk.','Forty-six percent of Saturday visitors could not attend weekday daytime programs.','Saturday comes after Friday.','The center offers several programs.'],'answer':'B. Forty-six percent of Saturday visitors could not attend weekday daytime programs.','why':'It directly shows that Saturday service reaches people blocked by weekday daytime schedules.'},
            {'text':'A manager says a new checklist reduced packing mistakes. Error logs fell from 31 errors in April to 18 in May after the checklist was introduced.','task':'Which detail is the strongest evidence?','choices':['The checklist is one page long.','The manager likes checklists.','Errors fell from 31 to 18 after introduction.','April and May are consecutive months.'],'answer':'C. Errors fell from 31 to 18 after introduction.','why':'It directly measures the outcome named in the claim.'},
            {'text':'A school proposes adding a covered bicycle rack because students avoid cycling when rain is expected.','task':'Which additional evidence would most directly support the claim?','choices':['A count of bicycles on rainy and dry school days.','The color of current bicycle racks.','The number of classrooms on campus.','A history of bicycle design.'],'answer':'A. A count of bicycles on rainy and dry school days.','why':'It tests whether rainy conditions are associated with lower bicycle use.'},
            {'text':'A report says a reminder message may reduce missed appointments.','task':'Which detail would be LEAST useful evidence?','choices':['Missed appointments before and after reminders began.','The percentage of clients who received the reminder.','A client comment saying the reminder helped them remember.','The paint color in the waiting room.'],'answer':'D. The paint color in the waiting room.','why':'It has no meaningful connection to whether reminders affect attendance.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A clinic argues that evening appointments help patients who cannot leave work during the day. The report includes total evening visits, a survey about work conflicts, patient satisfaction ratings, and the number of parking spaces.','task':'Which evidence is most direct?','choices':['The total number of evening visits.','The survey showing how many evening patients reported daytime work conflicts.','High satisfaction ratings.','The number of parking spaces.'],'answer':'B. The survey showing how many evening patients reported daytime work conflicts.','why':'The claim is specifically about work preventing daytime attendance; the survey measures that barrier directly.'},
            {'text':'A library says self-service pickup lockers reduce waiting at the service desk.','task':'Which evidence would best test that claim?','choices':['Locker color preferences.','Average service-desk wait times before and after locker use, measured during similar hours.','The number of books owned by the library.','A survey asking whether patrons like technology in general.'],'answer':'B. Average service-desk wait times before and after locker use, measured during similar hours.','why':'It directly measures the stated outcome while controlling for time-of-day differences.'},
            {'text':'A supervisor claims a short training video improved correct use of a safety form. The report says 93 percent watched the video, form errors declined, and a new form layout was introduced the same week.','task':'Which detail most directly supports improvement, while also showing why causation remains uncertain?','choices':['Ninety-three percent watched the video.','Form errors declined after the change, but the form layout changed at the same time.','The video was six minutes long.','Supervisors prefer video training.'],'answer':'B. Form errors declined after the change, but the form layout changed at the same time.','why':'The decline supports improvement, while the simultaneous redesign means the video cannot be isolated as the only cause.'},
            {'text':'A council member says a new crosswalk would help pedestrians reach a bus stop more safely.','task':'Which evidence package would be strongest?','choices':['Pedestrian counts, near-miss reports, and observations of where people currently cross.','A photograph of the bus color.','A list of nearby shop names.','A survey asking whether residents generally support public transportation.'],'answer':'A. Pedestrian counts, near-miss reports, and observations of where people currently cross.','why':'Together, those sources directly address use, risk, and crossing behavior at the proposed location.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'evidence-quality','slug':'evidence-quality','title':'Evidence Quality','skills':['R5.4','R5.5'],
        'goal':'Judge whether evidence is relevant to a claim and whether the available evidence is sufficient to support the strength and scope of that claim.',
        'explain':'Relevance asks whether evidence connects to the claim. Sufficiency asks whether there is enough high-quality evidence to justify the conclusion. Evidence can be relevant but still too limited, too narrow, or too confounded to support a broad claim.',
        'contrast':'Do not treat “related” as “enough.” One survey, one example, or one short pilot may be relevant but insufficient for a permanent or universal conclusion.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “Which evidence is most relevant?”, “Why is this evidence insufficient?”, “What additional evidence is needed?”, or “Which conclusion is best supported by the available evidence?”',
        'method':['Name the exact claim and its scope.','Check relevance: does the evidence address that claim directly?','Check sufficiency: is there enough evidence for how broad or certain the claim is?','Look for missing comparisons, small samples, short time periods, or competing causes.','Choose the strongest conclusion the evidence supports - no stronger.'],
        'example_text':'A company installed refill stations in one office. Bottle purchases declined during the same month, but the cafeteria also stopped selling one bottled-water brand.','example_q':'Why is the decline relevant but insufficient to prove the refill stations caused the change?','example_reason':'The decline relates to the claim, but another change happened at the same time. The refill stations cannot be isolated as the only cause.','example_answer':'Because the evidence is related to bottle use but does not rule out the cafeteria change as another cause.',
        'traps':['Assuming a large number is automatically strong evidence.','Ignoring the scope of the claim: one site does not automatically justify all sites.','Treating correlation as proof of causation.'],
        'diagnostic':['If you chose evidence only because it mentions the topic, ask whether it measures the actual claim.','If you jumped from a pilot to a permanent conclusion, compare the evidence scope with the claim scope.','If two changes happened together, ask whether the evidence isolates the proposed cause.'],
        'quick_text':'A four-week pilot reduced paper use in one department by 18 percent.','quick_q':'Does this prove the entire company should permanently adopt the policy?','quick_answer':'No. It is relevant evidence, but the short, single-department pilot is not sufficient by itself for a company-wide permanent conclusion.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'A school claims a new water station increases hydration. A survey shows students like the station\'s design.','task':'How should the survey be evaluated?','choices':['Relevant and sufficient.','Not very relevant to whether hydration increased.','Proof that dehydration ended.','Sufficient because surveys are always strong.'],'answer':'B. Not very relevant to whether hydration increased.','why':'Design preference does not measure drinking behavior or hydration.'},
            {'text':'A store says a new checkout layout reduces waiting. Average wait time fell by 90 seconds during a two-week pilot.','task':'Which statement is most accurate?','choices':['The evidence is relevant to waiting time, but more data may be needed before a permanent chain-wide change.','The evidence is irrelevant because it uses numbers.','The pilot proves every store will improve equally.','No conclusion at all can be drawn.'],'answer':'A. The evidence is relevant to waiting time, but more data may be needed before a permanent chain-wide change.','why':'It measures the claim directly, yet the scope and duration limit how broad the conclusion can be.'},
            {'text':'A manager claims a new scheduling app caused fewer late starts. Late starts declined, but the company also changed shift supervisors that week.','task':'Why is the evidence insufficient to establish causation?','choices':['Late starts are not measurable.','Another major change occurred at the same time.','Apps cannot affect schedules.','Supervisors never affect work.'],'answer':'B. Another major change occurred at the same time.','why':'The evidence does not isolate the app from the supervisor change.'},
            {'text':'A town wants to know whether a new shade structure reduces afternoon heat at a playground.','task':'Which evidence is most relevant?','choices':['Afternoon surface-temperature readings before and after installation.','The playground opening date.','The number of parks in the county.','A history of playground design.'],'answer':'A. Afternoon surface-temperature readings before and after installation.','why':'They directly measure the temperature outcome named in the claim.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A company reports that customer complaints fell 22 percent during a six-week chatbot pilot. Overall call volume also fell 19 percent during the same period.','task':'What is the best evaluation?','choices':['The complaint decline proves the chatbot improved service.','The complaint decline is irrelevant.','The decline is relevant, but lower call volume means more evidence is needed to know whether the chatbot caused the change.','A six-week pilot can never provide useful evidence.'],'answer':'C. The decline is relevant, but lower call volume means more evidence is needed to know whether the chatbot caused the change.','why':'The evidence relates to the claim, but a competing change could explain part of the decline.'},
            {'text':'A city tests protected bike lanes on one downtown street for three weekends and records fewer vehicle-bicycle conflicts.','task':'Which conclusion is best supported?','choices':['Protected bike lanes eliminate bicycle crashes everywhere.','The pilot suggests the design may reduce conflicts at that location, but broader evidence is needed before a citywide claim.','The test proves drivers prefer bicycles.','No useful conclusion can be drawn from the pilot.'],'answer':'B. The pilot suggests the design may reduce conflicts at that location, but broader evidence is needed before a citywide claim.','why':'It preserves both the useful result and the limited scope.'},
            {'text':'A school wants to know whether a later start time improves attendance. It compares attendance this semester with last semester, but this semester also introduced free morning buses.','task':'What additional evidence would most improve sufficiency?','choices':['Attendance patterns among similar students with and without access to the new bus service.','The school mascot.','A list of teacher birthdays.','The price of lunch.'],'answer':'A. Attendance patterns among similar students with and without access to the new bus service.','why':'It helps separate the effect of start time from the new transportation support.'},
            {'text':'A survey of 18 volunteers finds that 16 prefer a new park design. The city has 24,000 residents.','task':'Why is the survey not sufficient for a claim that “residents overwhelmingly support the redesign”?','choices':['Eighteen is an even number.','The sample is very small and may not represent the wider population.','Volunteer surveys are never relevant.','Preferences cannot be surveyed.'],'answer':'B. The sample is very small and may not represent the wider population.','why':'The main problem is representativeness and scope, not whether surveys can ever provide evidence.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'supported-unsupported-claims','slug':'supported-unsupported-claims','title':'Supported vs. Unsupported Claims','skills':['R5.6'],
        'goal':'Decide which claims are justified by the source evidence and which claims go beyond what the evidence can support.',
        'explain':'A supported claim matches the evidence in scope, certainty, and meaning. An unsupported claim may sound reasonable but add a cause, prediction, generalization, or level of certainty that the source does not establish.',
        'contrast':'“The pilot suggests the schedule may reduce absences” is different from “The schedule proves absences will fall everywhere.” Watch for words such as may, some, during the pilot, and at this site.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “Which claim is supported?”, “Which statement goes beyond the evidence?”, “Which revision is most defensible?”, or “Why is this conclusion too strong?”',
        'method':['List what the evidence actually shows.','Mark scope words: who, where, when, and how many.','Mark certainty words: may, suggests, proves, always, never.','Reject answers that add a new cause, prediction, or universal claim.','Choose the narrowest conclusion that fully fits the evidence.'],
        'example_text':'During an eight-week trial in one department, absences fell from 28 to 21 and completed work remained stable.','example_q':'Which conclusion is best supported?','example_reason':'The evidence supports a limited statement about this trial, not a universal claim about every workplace or permanent productivity gains.','example_answer':'The trial suggests the schedule may reduce absences in this department without reducing completed work.',
        'traps':['Turning “may” into “will.”','Generalizing from one group to all groups.','Adding a cause the source never isolated.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your answer uses “always,” “proves,” or “everyone,” compare that strength with the source wording.','If your answer mentions people or places not studied, you probably widened the scope.','If you added a reason for a result, check whether the source actually tested that cause.'],
        'quick_text':'A three-month pilot at one branch reduced average waiting time by 11 percent.','quick_q':'What is a supported claim?','quick_answer':'The pilot suggests the new process may reduce waiting time at that branch; it does not prove every branch will see the same result.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'In one six-week class pilot, students using a planning checklist submitted more assignments on time.','task':'Which claim is supported?','choices':['The checklist may have helped on-time submission in this class during the pilot.','The checklist permanently fixes procrastination for all students.','Every school should require the checklist.','The checklist caused higher exam scores.'],'answer':'A. The checklist may have helped on-time submission in this class during the pilot.','why':'It matches the group, time period, and measured outcome.'},
            {'text':'A community garden recorded more visitors after adding weekend workshops. The weather was also unusually mild.','task':'Which claim is unsupported?','choices':['Visitor numbers increased during the workshop period.','Weekend workshops definitely caused the entire increase.','Weather may also have influenced attendance.','More evidence is needed to isolate the cause.'],'answer':'B. Weekend workshops definitely caused the entire increase.','why':'The evidence does not isolate workshops from the unusually mild weather.'},
            {'text':'A pilot bus route carried 180 riders on its busiest Saturday.','task':'Which conclusion goes beyond the evidence?','choices':['At least one Saturday had 180 riders.','The route had substantial use on its busiest Saturday.','The route will carry 180 riders every Saturday.','The reported peak was 180 riders.'],'answer':'C. The route will carry 180 riders every Saturday.','why':'One peak day does not establish a recurring weekly result.'},
            {'text':'A survey of 300 residents finds 58 percent support a park renovation.','task':'Which wording is most defensible?','choices':['All residents support the renovation.','A majority of surveyed residents supported the renovation.','The renovation is guaranteed to succeed.','The survey proves every taxpayer will benefit.'],'answer':'B. A majority of surveyed residents supported the renovation.','why':'It accurately limits the claim to the people surveyed and the opinion measured.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A factory pilot reduced machine idle time by 9 percent for one production line while output stayed similar. The pilot lasted four weeks.','task':'Which claim is best supported?','choices':['The new process may reduce idle time on that line without reducing output during a short pilot.','The process will permanently raise profits across every factory.','The process caused workers to become more skilled.','The pilot proves all machines should use the process immediately.'],'answer':'A. The new process may reduce idle time on that line without reducing output during a short pilot.','why':'It preserves the measured outcome, group, duration, and uncertainty.'},
            {'text':'A town planted trees along one block. Summer surface temperatures on that block averaged 2°C lower than on a nearby unshaded block, but building materials also differed.','task':'Which statement is too strong?','choices':['The shaded block was cooler during the measurements.','Tree shade may have contributed to lower temperatures.','The data prove trees caused the entire temperature difference.','Building differences complicate the comparison.'],'answer':'C. The data prove trees caused the entire temperature difference.','why':'The comparison contains another meaningful difference, so the cause is not isolated.'},
            {'text':'A company survey found that 72 percent of respondents preferred flexible start times. Only 35 percent of employees completed the survey.','task':'Which conclusion is most defensible?','choices':['Most survey respondents preferred flexible starts, but the response rate limits claims about all employees.','Most employees definitely prefer flexible starts.','Flexible starts improve productivity.','The survey is useless because not everyone answered.'],'answer':'A. Most survey respondents preferred flexible starts, but the response rate limits claims about all employees.','why':'It reports the result while respecting the incomplete participation.'},
            {'text':'After a school added quiet-study rooms, library visits increased 14 percent. No data were collected on why students visited.','task':'Which claim needs additional evidence?','choices':['Library visits increased after the rooms were added.','The quiet rooms caused the entire increase in visits.','The school did not collect reasons for visits.','The increase was 14 percent.'],'answer':'B. The quiet rooms caused the entire increase in visits.','why':'Timing alone does not establish the reason for the increase.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'reasoning-assumptions','slug':'reasoning-assumptions','title':'Reasoning & Assumptions','skills':['R5.7','R5.8'],
        'goal':'Evaluate whether a conclusion follows logically from the evidence and identify hidden assumptions an argument depends on.',
        'explain':'Reasoning is the bridge between evidence and a claim. A weak argument may use real evidence but make a jump that the evidence does not justify. An assumption is an unstated idea that must be true for that jump to work.',
        'contrast':'Do not focus on naming formal fallacies. GED-style reasoning questions are usually about what is wrong in context: overgeneralizing, confusing correlation with cause, using a weak comparison, ignoring alternatives, or assuming a missing condition.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “What assumption does the argument depend on?”, “What is the flaw in the reasoning?”, “Which evidence weakens the conclusion?”, or “Which conclusion follows most logically?”',
        'method':['Write the evidence in one short line.','Write the conclusion in one short line.','Ask: what must be true to move from the evidence to that conclusion?','Look for alternative causes, weak comparisons, missing groups, or certainty jumps.','Choose the answer that identifies the exact reasoning gap.'],
        'example_text':'A manager says a booking app will eliminate waiting because restaurants use reservations successfully. The service desk, however, handles many requests whose length cannot be predicted in advance.','example_q':'What is the main reasoning problem?','example_reason':'The argument assumes a service desk works like a restaurant even though appointment length is much less predictable.','example_answer':'The comparison ignores an important difference between the two situations.',
        'traps':['Attacking the person instead of evaluating the reasoning.','Choosing an assumption that is possible but not necessary for the argument.','Calling an argument weak just because you disagree with the conclusion.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your answer describes a personality trait, return to the evidence-to-conclusion link.','If removing the assumption would not hurt the argument, it is probably not the required assumption.','If the evidence is true but the conclusion still feels too broad, identify the missing step.'],
        'quick_text':'“Our office should copy Company X\'s four-day schedule because Company X reported higher morale.”','quick_q':'What assumption is built into this reasoning?','quick_answer':'That the offices are similar enough, and that the schedule rather than other differences explains the reported morale result.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'“Three students who used the new study room earned higher grades, so the room raises grades for every student.”','task':'What is the main flaw?','choices':['The conclusion generalizes from a very small sample.','Grades cannot be measured.','Study rooms never help anyone.','The statement contains no conclusion.'],'answer':'A. The conclusion generalizes from a very small sample.','why':'Three students are not enough to justify a claim about every student.'},
            {'text':'“The café should stay open later because the gym is busy at night.”','task':'What assumption does this argument depend on?','choices':['Gym users are likely to want café service at that time.','The café has chairs.','Night comes after day.','All gym users drink coffee.'],'answer':'A. Gym users are likely to want café service at that time.','why':'Without a connection between nighttime gym users and café demand, the reasoning does not support later hours.'},
            {'text':'After a company changed its logo, sales increased. A manager says the new logo caused the increase.','task':'What evidence would most weaken the reasoning?','choices':['A major discount campaign began the same week.','The logo contains blue.','The company sells several products.','Sales are counted monthly.'],'answer':'A. A major discount campaign began the same week.','why':'It offers a plausible alternative cause for the sales increase.'},
            {'text':'“A restaurant uses reservations, so a hospital emergency room should also schedule every patient to eliminate waiting.”','task':'What is wrong with the comparison?','choices':['Emergency cases are less predictable than restaurant arrivals.','Restaurants and hospitals both have doors.','Reservations are always bad.','Hospitals never schedule appointments.'],'answer':'A. Emergency cases are less predictable than restaurant arrivals.','why':'The argument ignores a difference that matters directly to whether reservations can control waiting.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A council member says a new downtown parking fee will reduce traffic because one neighboring city saw traffic fall after adopting a similar fee. The neighboring city also opened a rail station that month.','task':'Which statement best identifies the reasoning weakness?','choices':['The argument assumes the parking fee caused the decline without separating the effect of the new rail station.','The neighboring city is mentioned by name.','Parking fees can never affect traffic.','Traffic data are always unreliable.'],'answer':'A. The argument assumes the parking fee caused the decline without separating the effect of the new rail station.','why':'The alternative change makes the single-cause conclusion uncertain.'},
            {'text':'A supervisor says, “If employees may choose one remote day, teamwork will collapse because people will stop communicating.”','task':'Which assumption is necessary for that prediction?','choices':['Remote work necessarily prevents effective communication.','Employees use computers.','The office has meeting rooms.','Some employees commute.'],'answer':'A. Remote work necessarily prevents effective communication.','why':'The prediction depends on treating remote work as incompatible with communication, which the statement does not establish.'},
            {'text':'A school compares its new reading program with a program used at a much smaller private school and assumes similar results will occur.','task':'What additional evidence would most strengthen the comparison?','choices':['Evidence that the schools have similar student populations, staffing, and implementation conditions.','The private school\'s mascot.','The age of the school buildings.','A list of unrelated extracurricular activities.'],'answer':'A. Evidence that the schools have similar student populations, staffing, and implementation conditions.','why':'A comparison is stronger when the factors relevant to the outcome are similar.'},
            {'text':'A manager argues that complaints fell after a new script was introduced, so the script should be used company-wide. The trial involved one experienced team for ten days.','task':'Which conclusion follows most reasonably?','choices':['The script may be promising, but a broader trial is needed before a company-wide conclusion.','The script has been proven universally effective.','Experienced teams should never test new tools.','Complaint data are irrelevant to customer service.'],'answer':'A. The script may be promising, but a broader trial is needed before a company-wide conclusion.','why':'It respects the positive result while recognizing the limited duration and group.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'credibility-counterarguments','slug':'credibility-counterarguments','title':'Credibility & Counterarguments','skills':['R5.9','R5.10'],
        'goal':'Judge whether a source is well positioned to support a specific claim and analyze how an argument acknowledges and responds to opposing views.',
        'explain':'Credibility is claim-specific. A source can be knowledgeable about one issue and weak on another. Counterarguments show what an opposing side says; a strong response answers the actual concern with relevant evidence or reasoning rather than ignoring or misrepresenting it.',
        'contrast':'Do not assume “official” or “expert” automatically means credible for every claim. Also distinguish a real rebuttal from simply repeating the original position.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “Which source is most credible for this claim?”, “Why is this source relevant?”, “How does the writer respond to the objection?”, or “Which response most strengthens the argument?”',
        'method':['Identify the exact claim the source is being used to support.','Ask what knowledge or access would be needed to support that claim.','Check whether the source has that expertise, data, or direct responsibility.','For counterarguments, state the objection fairly.','Check whether the response answers the objection with evidence, concession, limitation, or reasoning.'],
        'example_text':'A composting proposal cites the sanitation department for estimates of household food waste and a composting facility for processing capacity. A restaurant owner objects that collection bins will smell; the proposal responds with odor-complaint data from a sealed-bin pilot.','example_q':'Why are the cited sources useful?','example_reason':'Each source is matched to a claim it is positioned to know: waste volume, processing capacity, and odor performance.','example_answer':'Because the sources have direct responsibility or data relevant to the specific claims they support.',
        'traps':['Choosing a famous source whose expertise does not match the claim.','Treating one person\'s experience as proof of a citywide pattern.','Calling something a rebuttal when it never answers the opponent\'s actual concern.'],
        'diagnostic':['If you chose a source because of status alone, ask what specific information that source actually has.','If a response changes the subject, it has not answered the counterargument.','If the writer admits part of an objection but limits its importance with evidence, that can be a strong concession-and-response strategy.'],
        'quick_text':'A transit agency provides ridership counts; a local café owner predicts the route will increase citywide tax revenue.','quick_q':'Which source is better positioned to support a claim about ridership?','quick_answer':'The transit agency, because it directly collects ridership data; the café owner may offer local experience but not systemwide counts.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'A report asks how many emergency calls a fire department received last year.','task':'Which source is most credible for that claim?','choices':['The fire department\'s official call records.','A tourist blog.','A restaurant menu.','A student guessing from memory.'],'answer':'A. The fire department\'s official call records.','why':'The department directly records the information being asked about.'},
            {'text':'An article argues for later library hours. A critic says staffing costs may rise. The author replies with the actual overtime cost from a four-week pilot.','task':'What does the reply do?','choices':['It directly addresses the cost objection with relevant evidence.','It ignores the objection.','It changes the topic to book selection.','It proves staffing is free.'],'answer':'A. It directly addresses the cost objection with relevant evidence.','why':'The response answers the exact concern using measured cost data.'},
            {'text':'A company wants evidence about machine failure rates.','task':'Which source is most relevant?','choices':['Maintenance logs maintained by the engineering team.','A celebrity interview.','A customer comment about parking.','The cafeteria schedule.'],'answer':'A. Maintenance logs maintained by the engineering team.','why':'They directly record the failures being evaluated.'},
            {'text':'A writer says a new park plan may reduce parking spaces. An opponent says nearby shops will lose customers. The writer responds, “The park will look beautiful.”','task':'How strong is the response?','choices':['Weak, because it does not address the customer-loss concern.','Strong, because beauty always increases sales.','Strong, because it repeats the proposal.','Irrelevant only because parks are outdoors.'],'answer':'A. Weak, because it does not address the customer-loss concern.','why':'The response changes the subject instead of answering the economic objection.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'A city report cites a national environmental laboratory for water-quality testing methods and a neighborhood resident for the claim that “the river is safe for swimming everywhere.”','task':'Which evaluation is best?','choices':['Both sources are equally strong for both claims.','The laboratory is well matched to testing methods; one resident is weak evidence for a systemwide safety claim.','Residents can never provide useful evidence.','National laboratories know every local condition automatically.'],'answer':'B. The laboratory is well matched to testing methods; one resident is weak evidence for a systemwide safety claim.','why':'Credibility depends on the specific claim and the source\'s access to relevant evidence.'},
            {'text':'An employer proposes compressed workweeks. A critic says customer coverage may fall on Fridays. The employer replies that a pilot staggered employees\' off-days and Friday response time stayed within the normal range.','task':'Why is the response relatively strong?','choices':['It addresses the exact objection with pilot evidence rather than denying the concern abstractly.','It proves every future Friday will be identical.','It ignores customer coverage.','It relies only on employee preference.'],'answer':'A. It addresses the exact objection with pilot evidence rather than denying the concern abstractly.','why':'The response matches the objection and provides direct performance evidence.'},
            {'text':'A school board considers a new bus route. A transportation engineer estimates travel time using route data; a parent argues the route will “probably be unsafe” without citing any incident or design information.','task':'Which statement best evaluates the sources?','choices':['The engineer is stronger for travel-time estimates; the parent\'s safety concern may be worth investigating but is not yet well supported.','The parent is automatically stronger because parents care about safety.','Both claims are equally supported.','Engineers cannot evaluate routes.'],'answer':'A. The engineer is stronger for travel-time estimates; the parent\'s safety concern may be worth investigating but is not yet well supported.','why':'It distinguishes source fit from the importance of the concern.'},
            {'text':'A proposal admits that a new collection program will cost more in its first year, then argues that equipment is a one-time purchase and provides three-year cost projections.','task':'What strategy is the writer using?','choices':['Conceding part of the objection and responding with longer-term evidence.','Ignoring the objection.','Attacking the opponent personally.','Changing the subject to an unrelated benefit.'],'answer':'A. Conceding part of the objection and responding with longer-term evidence.','why':'The writer acknowledges the short-term cost instead of denying it, then limits its force with relevant projections.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'compare-sources-arguments','slug':'compare-sources-arguments','title':'Compare Sources & Arguments','skills':['R6.1','R6.2'],
        'goal':'Compare what two sources agree and disagree about, how they use evidence, and which argument is better supported.',
        'explain':'When two sources address the same issue, compare them on the same dimension: claim, evidence, reasoning, purpose, or recommendation. A stronger argument is not the one you personally prefer; it is the one better supported by relevant evidence and sound reasoning.',
        'contrast':'Do not compare a claim from Source A with an unrelated detail from Source B. Do not choose the side you agree with before evaluating the support.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “What do both sources agree on?”, “How do their recommendations differ?”, “How does Source B challenge Source A?”, or “Which argument is better supported and why?”',
        'method':['State Source A\'s claim and Source B\'s claim.','List the evidence each source uses.','Identify one point of agreement and one point of disagreement.','Compare evidence quality and reasoning on the same issue.','Choose the better-supported argument, not the more familiar opinion.'],
        'example_text':'Source A supports extending library hours permanently based on a student survey. Source B supports a shorter pilot, noting that the survey measured preference but did not measure staffing cost or actual late-night attendance.','example_q':'How does Source B challenge Source A?','example_reason':'Source B does not deny that students want later hours; it argues that preference data alone are insufficient for a permanent schedule decision.','example_answer':'It questions whether Source A\'s survey is sufficient evidence for a permanent change.',
        'traps':['Choosing agreement because both sources mention the same topic.','Comparing different kinds of evidence as if they answer the same question.','Treating confidence or strong language as stronger support.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your answer could describe only one source, you may not actually be comparing them.','If you picked a winner before listing each source\'s evidence, restart with the evidence.','If both sources use data, ask which data more directly address the disputed claim.'],
        'quick_text':'Source A wants a permanent weekend route; Source B wants a three-month pilot before deciding. Both agree Sunday workers have limited transport.','quick_q':'What do the sources agree on, and where do they differ?','quick_answer':'They agree there is a transport-access problem; they differ on whether the evidence is strong enough for a permanent route now.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'Source A says the park needs more lighting because evening use has increased. Source B agrees evening use has increased but recommends testing temporary lights before permanent installation.','task':'What do both sources agree on?','choices':['Evening park use has increased.','Permanent lights should be installed immediately.','Temporary lights are useless.','The park should close earlier.'],'answer':'A. Evening park use has increased.','why':'Both sources accept the same underlying condition even though their recommendations differ.'},
            {'text':'Source A supports a permanent café extension. Source B supports a four-week trial first.','task':'What is the main difference?','choices':['Whether the café exists.','How quickly the schedule should become permanent.','Whether customers buy food.','Whether four weeks contains weekends.'],'answer':'B. How quickly the schedule should become permanent.','why':'The disagreement is about the decision strength and timing, not the general topic.'},
            {'text':'Source A cites a preference survey. Source B says preference does not show whether the program is affordable.','task':'How does Source B challenge Source A?','choices':['By arguing that the evidence does not address cost.','By denying that surveys exist.','By changing the subject to weather.','By proving the program is unaffordable.'],'answer':'A. By arguing that the evidence does not address cost.','why':'Source B identifies a mismatch between the evidence and a separate decision criterion.'},
            {'text':'Source A predicts remote work will reduce teamwork. Source B cites a six-month trial in which project completion and response times remained stable.','task':'Which source is better supported on performance?','choices':['Source A, because predictions are stronger than trials.','Source B, because it provides measured results from an actual trial.','Both are equally supported because both mention work.','Neither can make any statement about performance.'],'answer':'B. Source B, because it provides measured results from an actual trial.','why':'The trial directly measures performance; the prediction is unsupported.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'Source A argues for extending bus service permanently because 62 percent of survey respondents want later service. Source B agrees late service may help but notes the survey had a 28 percent response rate and recommends a trial measuring actual ridership.','task':'Which comparison is most accurate?','choices':['Both sources reject later service.','Source A emphasizes stated preference; Source B questions whether preference data are sufficient for a permanent decision.','Source B proves nobody wants later service.','Source A and B use identical evidence and reach identical conclusions.'],'answer':'B. Source A emphasizes stated preference; Source B questions whether preference data are sufficient for a permanent decision.','why':'It captures both the shared issue and the difference in evidence standards.'},
            {'text':'Source A says a school should replace printed textbooks with tablets because tablets are lighter. Source B notes that weight is a benefit but provides repair-cost and access data showing some students lack reliable home internet.','task':'How does Source B strengthen the discussion?','choices':['It adds decision factors that Source A\'s single benefit does not address.','It proves tablets are always harmful.','It rejects the fact that tablets are lighter.','It discusses an unrelated topic.'],'answer':'A. It adds decision factors that Source A\'s single benefit does not address.','why':'Source B broadens the evidence relevant to the decision without denying the original benefit.'},
            {'text':'Source A cites one nearby city where a policy succeeded. Source B cites outcomes from twelve cities, including conditions under which the policy failed.','task':'Which argument has stronger comparative support?','choices':['Source A, because one example is easier to remember.','Source B, because the broader evidence tests the policy across more conditions and acknowledges limits.','Both are automatically equal because both cite cities.','Neither source can use examples.'],'answer':'B. Source B, because the broader evidence tests the policy across more conditions and acknowledges limits.','why':'The larger, more varied evidence base better supports a general policy judgment.'},
            {'text':'Source A claims a program will save money but gives no cost estimate. Source B gives startup and annual operating costs and identifies where savings would need to occur.','task':'Which statement best explains why Source B is better supported?','choices':['It uses more words.','It provides evidence directly related to the financial claim and makes the required tradeoff visible.','It agrees with every reader.','It avoids numbers.'],'answer':'B. It provides evidence directly related to the financial claim and makes the required tradeoff visible.','why':'Argument strength comes from relevant support and reasoning, not length or tone.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'text-data-formats','slug':'text-data-formats','title':'Text, Data & Different Formats','skills':['R6.3','R6.4'],
        'goal':'Integrate information from prose, tables, charts, fact sheets, timelines, or other formats and determine whether the formats support, qualify, or contradict each other.',
        'explain':'Different formats may answer different parts of the same question. A paragraph may make a claim while a table shows whether the pattern exists. Good readers translate both formats into the same comparison before drawing a conclusion.',
        'contrast':'Do not treat a table as decoration or assume one striking number represents the whole pattern. Also do not force two formats to agree when the data actually qualify the prose.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “How does the table support the article?”, “Which claim is supported by the data?”, “What does the fact sheet add?”, or “Which statement accurately combines both formats?”',
        'method':['Identify the claim or question in the prose.','Read table/visual labels before reading individual numbers.','Compare the correct rows, columns, dates, or categories.','State what the second format adds: support, detail, limitation, or contradiction.','Choose a conclusion that uses both formats without overstating either one.'],
        'example_text':'An article says shuttle crowding is concentrated between 8:00 and 8:30 a.m. A table shows average passenger loads of 42 at 7:30, 61 at 8:00, 67 at 8:20, and 44 at 9:00 on buses designed for 55 riders.','example_q':'How does the table relate to the claim?','example_reason':'The two peak-time rows exceed capacity, while the earlier and later rows do not. The data support a concentrated peak rather than all-morning crowding.','example_answer':'It supports the claim that crowding is concentrated around 8:00-8:30 a.m.',
        'traps':['Reading the largest number without checking its label.','Using one format while ignoring a limitation in the other.','Saying the data prove a broader claim than the categories actually cover.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your answer mentions a number but not what it measures, return to the labels.','If the prose says “often” and your answer says “always,” you probably overstated the combined evidence.','If formats appear to conflict, check whether they are measuring different groups or time periods.'],
        'quick_text':'A memo says complaints are highest on Mondays. A table shows 34 Monday complaints, 18 Tuesday, 17 Wednesday, 16 Thursday, and 19 Friday.','quick_q':'What does the table contribute?','quick_answer':'It supports the memo by showing Monday has the highest count among the listed weekdays.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'Article: “The noon shuttle is the busiest.” Table: 10 a.m. 28 riders; 11 a.m. 35; noon 52; 1 p.m. 39.','task':'How does the table relate to the article?','choices':['It supports the claim.','It contradicts the claim.','It provides no relevant information.','It proves every noon shuttle is full.'],'answer':'A. It supports the claim.','why':'Noon has the highest rider count in the displayed data.'},
            {'text':'Memo: “Most errors occur during the final packing step.” Table: Receiving 8; Assembly 11; Packing 27; Shipping 9.','task':'Which claim is supported by the table?','choices':['Packing has the highest recorded error count.','All packing work is incorrect.','Shipping has no errors.','Receiving causes packing errors.'],'answer':'A. Packing has the highest recorded error count.','why':'The table supports a comparative count, not a universal or causal claim.'},
            {'text':'Article: “The pilot lowered waiting time.” Fact sheet: Average wait before pilot 18 min; during pilot 13 min; sample: four weekdays.','task':'What limitation does the fact sheet add?','choices':['The result covers only four weekdays.','Waiting time cannot be measured.','The pilot increased waiting.','The article contains no claim.'],'answer':'A. The result covers only four weekdays.','why':'The fact sheet supports the decline but shows the evidence comes from a limited period.'},
            {'text':'Timeline: May - staff training; June - new form launched; July - errors decline. Memo: “The new form alone caused the decline.”','task':'What does the timeline show?','choices':['Another change occurred before the decline, so the form cannot be isolated as the only cause.','The memo is automatically correct.','Training occurred after the decline.','Errors did not decline.'],'answer':'A. Another change occurred before the decline, so the form cannot be isolated as the only cause.','why':'The timeline introduces another plausible contributing factor.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'Article: “Crowding is mainly an 8 a.m. problem.” Table: 7:30 - 48/55 seats; 8:00 - 68/55; 8:20 - 64/55; 9:00 - 50/55. Passenger note: “I was left behind twice last month around 8:15.”','task':'Which statement best integrates all three sources?','choices':['Crowding appears concentrated near 8:00-8:20, and the passenger note gives an example consistent with the capacity data.','The passenger note proves every rider is left behind daily.','Crowding is identical at every time.','The table contradicts the article because 7:30 also has riders.'],'answer':'A. Crowding appears concentrated near 8:00-8:20, and the passenger note gives an example consistent with the capacity data.','why':'It combines the pattern with the anecdote while keeping the anecdote in proper scope.'},
            {'text':'Proposal: “The new printer reduces cost.” Cost table: Old printer $0.041/page; new printer $0.029/page; new maintenance contract adds $180/month.','task':'What must be known before the overall cost claim is supported?','choices':['Monthly print volume, so per-page savings can be compared with the added maintenance cost.','The printer color.','The office wall size.','Whether employees like new equipment.'],'answer':'A. Monthly print volume, so per-page savings can be compared with the added maintenance cost.','why':'The formats provide both a saving and a new expense; volume is needed to combine them.'},
            {'text':'Article: “Weekend use increased after the schedule change.” Table: Saturday visits rose 22%; Sunday visits fell 4%; total weekend visits rose 9%.','task':'Which statement accurately combines the article and table?','choices':['Overall weekend use increased, but the change was driven by Saturday rather than both days.','Both Saturday and Sunday use increased equally.','Sunday visits caused the entire increase.','The article is false because one day declined.'],'answer':'A. Overall weekend use increased, but the change was driven by Saturday rather than both days.','why':'It respects both the total and the different day-level patterns.'},
            {'text':'Fact sheet: 74% of survey respondents support a program; response rate 31%. Article: “Residents strongly support the program.”','task':'What is the best evaluation?','choices':['The fact sheet supports strong support among respondents but does not establish the same level of support among all residents.','The article is fully proven for all residents.','The survey contains no useful information.','A response rate cannot affect interpretation.'],'answer':'A. The fact sheet supports strong support among respondents but does not establish the same level of support among all residents.','why':'The combined reading must preserve the difference between respondents and the full population.'},
        ]
    },
    {
        'id':'synthesize-sources','slug':'synthesize-sources','title':'Synthesize Across Sources','skills':['R6.5'],
        'goal':'Combine information from two or more sources to form a new conclusion that no single source fully provides on its own.',
        'explain':'Synthesis is not a list of summaries. You use one source together with another to answer a shared question, resolve a tradeoff, or draw a conclusion that depends on both pieces of information.',
        'contrast':'Do not choose an answer supported by only one source when the question asks for combined evidence. Do not invent a compromise that sounds sensible but is not supported.',
        'wording':'Questions may ask: “Which conclusion is best supported by both sources?”, “What can be concluded when the sources are considered together?”, “Which plan is best supported by all the evidence?”, or “Which statement synthesizes the sources without overstating them?”',
        'method':['Summarize each source in one line.','Identify the shared decision or question.','Mark where the sources support, limit, or extend each other.','Build a conclusion that requires information from more than one source.','Check that every part of the conclusion has source support.'],
        'example_text':'Source 1 shows courtyard temperatures are highest near the west wall after 2 p.m. Source 2 shows a full shade structure is expensive and cannot be installed this year. Source 3 shows portable shade can be installed quickly but covers less area.','example_q':'What conclusion is best supported by all three sources?','example_reason':'The heat data identify the urgent location, while the cost and timing data make a phased response more practical than a full immediate build.','example_answer':'Use portable shade at the hottest area now while planning a larger permanent solution later.',
        'traps':['Repeating Source 1 and ignoring Source 2.','Choosing the most ambitious plan even when timing or cost evidence contradicts it.','Inventing a conclusion based on outside knowledge rather than the supplied sources.'],
        'diagnostic':['If your answer can be supported without reading one of the sources, it may not be true synthesis.','If one source gives a limitation, your final conclusion should usually reflect that limitation.','If your conclusion adds a new fact, identify which source actually supports it.'],
        'quick_text':'Source A says the north entrance has the highest pedestrian count. Source B says only the north entrance has room for a ramp without moving utilities.','quick_q':'What conclusion can be synthesized?','quick_answer':'If the goal is to place one ramp where it can serve many users without utility relocation, the north entrance has support from both sources.',
        'wb1':[
            {'text':'Source A: The east classroom wing is hottest after noon. Source B: Portable shades can be installed there before next month; permanent trees will take years to provide full shade.','task':'Which conclusion uses both sources?','choices':['Portable shade could address the hottest wing sooner while longer-term shade develops.','Trees are always better than portable shade.','The west wing is hottest.','No action is possible.'],'answer':'A. Portable shade could address the hottest wing sooner while longer-term shade develops.','why':'It combines the location finding from Source A with the timing information from Source B.'},
            {'text':'Source A: Most missed appointments occur on Monday mornings. Source B: Reminder messages are currently sent only the evening before appointments.','task':'Which combined question would be most useful to investigate next?','choices':['Whether earlier or additional reminders reduce Monday morning misses.','What color the clinic walls are.','Whether Tuesday has a name.','How many chairs are in the lobby.'],'answer':'A. Whether earlier or additional reminders reduce Monday morning misses.','why':'It uses the timing pattern from one source and the current reminder process from the other.'},
            {'text':'Source A: A bridge repair will take four months if fully closed. Source B: The detour adds 18 minutes for school buses.','task':'Which conclusion is supported by both?','choices':['A full closure would create a substantial transportation impact for school buses.','The bridge should never be repaired.','All drivers use school buses.','The detour is shorter than the normal route.'],'answer':'A. A full closure would create a substantial transportation impact for school buses.','why':'The repair duration and detour time together show a prolonged impact.'},
            {'text':'Source A: Survey respondents prefer more weekend library hours. Source B: Saturday usage is much higher than Sunday usage.','task':'Which plan is best supported?','choices':['Test additional Saturday hours before assuming both weekend days need the same extension.','Extend Sunday only because it is less busy.','Close on Saturday.','Make a permanent seven-day schedule immediately.'],'answer':'A. Test additional Saturday hours before assuming both weekend days need the same extension.','why':'The survey supports more weekend access while the usage pattern suggests Saturday is the stronger starting point.'},
        ],
        'wb2':[
            {'text':'Source 1: Flooding occurs most often near the western trail entrance. Source 2: Raising the full trail would cost $2.4 million; improving drainage at the western entrance would cost $340,000. Source 3: Engineers say drainage work would reduce minor floods but not protect against rare major storms.','task':'Which conclusion best synthesizes all three?','choices':['Targeted drainage is a lower-cost way to reduce frequent minor flooding at the main trouble spot, but it is not a complete solution for major storms.','Drainage will eliminate every flood.','The full trail must be raised immediately because cost does not matter.','The western entrance is never flooded.'],'answer':'A. Targeted drainage is a lower-cost way to reduce frequent minor flooding at the main trouble spot, but it is not a complete solution for major storms.','why':'It combines location, cost, and effectiveness while preserving the limitation.'},
            {'text':'Source 1: A device-lending program had high demand during exam weeks. Source 2: Repair logs show the oldest devices have twice the failure rate of newer ones. Source 3: The budget can replace only one-third of the fleet this year.','task':'Which plan is best supported?','choices':['Prioritize replacing the highest-failure devices while maintaining lending capacity during peak weeks.','Replace every device immediately regardless of condition.','Stop lending devices during exams.','Use the budget only for advertising.'],'answer':'A. Prioritize replacing the highest-failure devices while maintaining lending capacity during peak weeks.','why':'The conclusion integrates demand, reliability, and budget limits.'},
            {'text':'Source 1: Residents report noise mainly after 10 p.m. Source 2: Delivery companies save the most travel time between 8 and 11 p.m. Source 3: Quiet-equipment trials cut unloading noise substantially but do not remove it completely.','task':'Which policy is most strongly supported?','choices':['Allow limited evening deliveries with quiet-equipment requirements and evaluate noise near residences before extending later.','Require all deliveries after midnight.','Ban deliveries at every hour.','Assume quiet equipment removes all concerns.'],'answer':'A. Allow limited evening deliveries with quiet-equipment requirements and evaluate noise near residences before extending later.','why':'It balances the travel-time benefit with the timing of complaints and the partial effectiveness of quieter equipment.'},
            {'text':'Source 1: A school roof can hold solar panels on only the south wing. Source 2: The south wing uses 38 percent of campus electricity. Source 3: The proposed system would supply about 24 percent of south-wing demand.','task':'Which statement synthesizes the evidence without overstating it?','choices':['Solar panels on the south wing could offset part of that wing\'s electricity use but would not power the whole campus.','Solar panels would eliminate the school\'s electricity bill.','The north wing should receive the panels.','The south wing uses all campus electricity.'],'answer':'A. Solar panels on the south wing could offset part of that wing\'s electricity use but would not power the whole campus.','why':'It combines structural feasibility, energy share, and expected output at the correct scope.'},
        ]
    },
]
